{"records":[{"id":"c696dvvynf","title":"Genetic determinants of host tropism in Klebsiella phages","description":"Bacteriophages play key roles in bacterial ecology and evolution and are potential antimicrobials. However, the determinants of phage-host specificity remain elusive. Here, we isolate 46 phages to challenge 138 representative clinical isolates of Klebsiella pneumoniae, a widespread opportunistic pathogen. Spot tests show a narrow host range for most phages, with <2% of 6,319 phage-host combinations tested yielding detectable interactions. Bacterial capsule diversity is the main factor restricting phage host range. Consequently, phage-encoded depolymerases are key determinants of host tropism, and depolymerase sequence types are associated with the ability to infect specific capsular types across phage families. However, all phages with a broader host range found do not encode canonical depolymerases, suggesting alternative modes of entry. These findings expand our knowledge of the complex interactions between bacteria and their viruses and point out the feasibility of predicting the first steps of phage infection using bacterial and phage genome sequences.\n\nList of contents:\n\n1. Tables: \n   1.1 Depolymerase_DB (used to detect Dpos in phages)\n   1.2 Klebsiella_phages_review (Phages from the literature with known capsular tropism)\n   1.3 Phage_killing_curves (raw data of bactericidal assays performed)\n   1.4. Phage_receptors (list of candidate bacterial receptors) \n\n2. Scripts: \nTo obtain the True Positive Rate (TPR) of bacterial and phage traits to predict strain spotting and phage capsular tropism, respectively. \n\n3. PDB files of predicted receptor binding proteins (RBPs) of phages\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-01-27T14:48:27.339Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/c696dvvynf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/c696dvvynf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Beatriz","last_name":"Beamud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Neris","last_name":"García-González","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mar","last_name":"Gómez","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fernando","last_name":"Gonzalez Candelas","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pilar","last_name":"Domingo-Calap","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rafael","last_name":"Sanjuan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microbiology","Evolutionary Biology","Genomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y8dndzn3mg","title":"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication","description":"These data are part of the Supplemental Items associated with the manuscript entitled \"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication\". These files include Supplementary Data file 1-4.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-16T10:34:13.579Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y8dndzn3mg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y8dndzn3mg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Limeng","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Screening","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"v4zbbbsr5t","title":"VPS33A and VPS18 orchestrate porcine epidemic diarrhea virus replication by modulating autophagic flux","description":"Completion of autophagy through autophagosome-lysosome fusion represents a critical regulatory checkpoint that is frequently manipulated during viral infection, yet how coronaviruses exploit this terminal step remains incompletely understood. Porcine epidemic diarrhea virus (PEDV), a highly pathogenic alphacoronavirus, has been reported to induce autophagy, but whether and how complete autophagic flux contributes to viral replication is unclear. Here, using a genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 knockout screen in Vero cells, we identify the HOPS complex subunits VPS33A and VPS18 as essential host factors for PEDV infection. Genetic ablation of either VPS33A or VPS18 profoundly suppresses viral progeny production and arrests autophagic flux at the autophagosome stage, demonstrating that PEDV replication is associated with HOPS-mediated autolysosome formation. Domain-mapping analyses further reveal that the structural integrity of VPS33A and the α-solenoid plus RING domains of VPS18 are indispensable for both autophagosome-lysosome fusion and efficient viral replication. Mechanistically, we show that PEDV non-structural proteins nsp3 and nsp4 physically interact with VPS33A and VPS18. While either protein alone initiates autophagosome formation, their coordinated action contributes to drive complete autophagy in a HOPS-dependent manner. Collectively, our findings uncover a coronavirus strategy that actively promotes autophagic completion through VPS33A and VPS18, two core subunits of the HOPS complex, to support replication, thereby establishing autolysosome formation as a critical host process exploited by PEDV.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-05-18T15:12:38.75Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/v4zbbbsr5t","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/v4zbbbsr5t"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"liang","last_name":"guo","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"the National Key Research and Development Program of China","grant_id":"2023YFC3404301, 2023YFF1000204, and 2021YFA0805901"},{"id":null,"name":"the Hainan Provincial Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"323QN261"}],"categories":["Virus","Animal"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"jgvcfbtcwc","title":"VI-PPI network","description":"Rawdata for <<PNP is a novel anti-influenza host target regulating viral replication and host hyper-inflammation via purine salvage>>","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-07-19T18:07:04.936Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jgvcfbtcwc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jgvcfbtcwc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"æå®","last_name":"æ","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mouse"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"jjb3n649c6","title":"Interplay of Host and Viral Genetic Variations in Modulating Antibody Responses to Genotype 3a Hepatitis C Virus: Implications for Vaccine Design","description":"This study investigates the complex interplay between host and viral genetics that influence antibody responses to Hepatitis C Virus (HCV), with a focus on the understudied genotype 3a. By integrating viral whole-genome sequencing, robust binding and neutralization assays, and analysis of host factors for 54 patients with HCV gt3a infection, we identified novel viral polymorphisms and glycosylation motifs across HCV gt3a E1 and E2 associated with antibody responses. To be specific, we discovered two sites in or near the CD81 binding sites in E2 (sites 501 and 533) that were associated with neutralization sensitivity, along with an additional site in E2 (653) that was associated with binding. Additionally, motifs at two glycosylation sites (N476 and N234) were associated with binding and/or neutralisation. Furthermore, an increase in intra-patient hypervariable region 1 (HVR1) diversity was associated with stronger binding response. Our findings also highlight the importance of considering host factors, such as genetic variation in IFNL4 gene, in vaccine development and deployment strategies. Specifically, the IFNλ4-P70 variant, which exhibits reduced binding compared to the IFNλ4-Null variant, should be taken into account.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-01-28T15:32:05.778Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jjb3n649c6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jjb3n649c6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhiqing","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Isla","last_name":"Humphreys","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jocelyn","last_name":"Quistrebert","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Haiting","last_name":"Chai","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Josh ","last_name":"Dhir","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alexandru","last_name":"Nisioi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Paul ","last_name":"Radford","orcid":"0000-0002-0314-0346"},{"first_name":"Jonathan ","last_name":"Ball","orcid":null},{"first_name":"William","last_name":"Irving","orcid":"0000-0002-7268-3168"},{"first_name":"Paul","last_name":"Klenerman","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Eleanor ","last_name":"Barnes","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jane","last_name":"McKeating","orcid":"0000-0002-7229-5886"},{"first_name":"Alexander","last_name":"Tarr","orcid":"0000-0003-1009-0823"},{"first_name":"Azim","last_name":"Ansari","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genetics","Immunology","Infectious Disease","Vaccine","Clinical Medicine","Hepatitis C"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5v9p64kmr7","title":"YIPF5 orchestrates the maturation of PRRSV replication organelles by bridging the C-shaped intermediate to spherical DMV transition","description":"The biogenesis of double-membrane vesicles (DMVs) is a fundamental yet poorly understood process in the replication of nidoviruses. Here, we identify the host factor YIPF5 as a critical spatiotemporal organizer that orchestrates the maturation of PRRSV replication organelles. By integrating functional assays with advanced microscopy, we demonstrate that PRRSV hijacks YIPF5 to anchor nsp2/3 complexes at the perinuclear region. Mechanistically, we define a two-stage assembly model: nsp2-nsp3 complexes initiate membrane curvature to form zippered intermediates, while YIPF5 acts as an essential scaffold to drive the structural transition from C-shaped intermediates to mature, spherical DMVs. Our findings delineate a conserved regulatory checkpoint in nidovirus replication and position the YIPF5-nsp3 axis as a potential target for broad-spectrum antiviral strategies.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-07-21T09:27:23.849Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5v9p64kmr7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5v9p64kmr7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xingyu","last_name":"Yan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"liang","last_name":"guo","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Host Factor"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7fgg5452pr","title":"The SARS-CoV-2 interactome","description":"SARS-CoV-2 is an RNA virus whose success as a pathogen relies on its abilities to repurpose host RNA-binding proteins (RBPs) and to evade antiviral RBPs. To uncover the SARS-CoV-2 RNA interactome, we here develop a robust ribonucleoprotein (RNP) capture protocol and identify 109 host factors that directly bind to SARS-CoV-2 RNAs. Applying RNP capture on another coronavirus HCoV-OC43 revealed evolutionarily conserved interactions between coronaviral RNAs and host proteins. Transcriptome analyses and knockdown experiments delineated 17 antiviral RBPs including ZC3HAV1, TRIM25, PARP12, and SHFL and 8 proviral RBPs such as EIF3D and CSDE1 which are responsible for co-opting multiple steps of the mRNA life cycle. This also led to the identification of LARP1, a downstream target of the mTOR signaling pathway, as an antiviral host factor that interacts with the SARS-CoV-2 RNAs. Overall, this study provides a comprehensive list of RBPs regulating coronaviral replication and opens new avenues for therapeutic interventions.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-05-07T07:11:04.995Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7fgg5452pr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7fgg5452pr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sungyul","last_name":"Lee","orcid":"0000-0003-3207-1199"},{"first_name":"Young-suk","last_name":"Lee","orcid":"0000-0003-0220-9951"},{"first_name":"Yeon","last_name":"Choi","orcid":"0000-0003-4983-6573"},{"first_name":"Aheyon","last_name":"Son","orcid":"0000-0001-7945-3851"},{"first_name":"Youngran","last_name":"Park","orcid":"0000-0002-8621-1399"},{"first_name":"Kyung-Min","last_name":"Lee","orcid":"0000-0002-1373-5407"},{"first_name":"Jeesoo","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jong-Seo","last_name":"Kim","orcid":"0000-0002-8909-8440"},{"first_name":"V. Narry","last_name":"Kim","orcid":"0000-0002-3213-4965"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Proteomics","RNA","Interactome","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ryr859j6pr","title":"Viral fitness landscapes in diverse host species reveal multiple evolutionary lines for the NS1 gene of influenza A viruses - Supplemental Data (Part 2)","description":"IAV NS1 protein is a multifunctional virulence factor that inhibits the type-I interferon host response through a myriad of mechanisms. How NS1 has evolved to enable this remarkable property across species and its specific impact in the overall replication, pathogenicity and host preference, remains unknown. We analyzed the NS1 evolutionary landscape and host tropism through the use of a barcoded library of recombinant IAV.  We designed a barcoded viral library of recombinant influenza A viruses expressing a selection of NS1 from different strains, hosts, countries and years. Each virus was individually rescued and deep-sequenced using the Illumina MiSeq platform to verify viral genome integrity and sequence. Results showed diverse and apparently random evolutionary pathways taken by IAV NS1 according to its multiple phylogenetic lineages. In summary, the high evolutionary plasticity of this viral protein underscores the ability of IAV to adapt to multiple hosts and aids in our understanding of its global prevalence.\n\nThis dataset contains the following:\n\n1. Illumina deep-sequencing raw data of each virus included in the final library (barcodes 124-128).\n2. Illumina deep-sequencing raw data of the barcode analysis in infection experiments.\n3-6. Custom scripts for different data analysis in the manuscript.\n7. Segment sequences of WT Influenza A viruses (A/Puerto Rico/08/1934 and A/Vietnam/1203/04) used in this work.\n8. Full sequences of empty vectors (pCAGGS, pPolI and pDZ) used in this work.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-10-22T02:44:17.229Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ryr859j6pr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ryr859j6pr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Raquel","last_name":"Munoz-Moreno","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Carles","last_name":"Martinez-Romero","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Blanco-Melo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christian V.","last_name":"Forst","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Raffael","last_name":"Nachbagauer","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Asiel Arturo","last_name":"Benitez","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nacho","last_name":"Mena","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sadaf","last_name":"Aslam","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Vinod","last_name":"Balasubramaniam","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ilseob","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Maryline","last_name":"Panis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"David","last_name":"Sachs","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Man-Seong","last_name":"Park","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Florian","last_name":"Krammer","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Benjamin R.","last_name":"tenOever","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Adolfo","last_name":"Garcia-Sastre","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virology","Illumina MiSeq"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2r2r9484gp","title":"TMEM41B is a pan-flavivirus host factor. Hoffmann et al.","description":"Table S1_oligos","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-12-03T08:58:20.258Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2r2r9484gp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2r2r9484gp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"William","last_name":"Schneider","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Coronavirus","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2rgtv8vsg4","title":"Residues E124, K129, and R134 in eEF1A Domain I Promote ALV‑J Replication via direct interaction with reverse transcriptase","description":"Subgroup J avian leukosis virus (ALV-J) is an oncogenic retrovirus posing significant threats to the global poultry industry. While host factors are known to regulate retroviral reverse transcription, the specific host proteins that support this critical step in ALV infection have remained completely unknown. In this study, we confirmed that overexpression or knockdown of eEF1A significantly enhanced or suppressed ALV-J proliferation, respectively. Co-immunoprecipitation and molecular docking demonstrated a direct interaction between eEF1A and the ALV-J reverse transcriptase (RT). Furthermore, three conserved residues in eEF1A domain I, including glutamate 124 (E124), lysine 129 (K129) and arginine 134 (R134), were identified as critical for eEF1A-RT interaction. Alanine substitution at these EKR residues disrupted eEF1A-RT binding, markedly impairing viral reverse transcription and infectious virion production. From the viral perspective, a conserved tryptophan (W250) in the RT thumb domain acts as the key counterpart to the eEF1A EKR motif, and its mutation similarly abolishes the interaction and viral replication. Notably, this eEF1A-RT interaction and its functional significance are conserved across ALV subgroups A, B, K, and J. Together, our work elucidate that three residues E124, K129, and R134 within domain I of eEF1A mediate the direct interaction with ALV-J RT, promoting viral replication by enhancing reverse transcription efficiency. This finding provides the first mechanistic insight into host‑dependent regulation of reverse transcription in ALV infection, broaden the understanding of how retroviruses co‑opt host factors to facilitate this critical process, and provide a rational basis for genome editing of eEF1A to generate broad‑spectrum ALV‑resistant poultry lines.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-11T06:13:59.244Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2rgtv8vsg4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2rgtv8vsg4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"sheng","last_name":"chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qingmei","last_name":"Xie","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virology","Veterinary Microbiology"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":141074,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":287,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":141074,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":140300,"restricted":70,"embargoed":704},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2304},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":713},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":472},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":440},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":436},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":420},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":400},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":341},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":318},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":274}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":138423},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1905},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":185},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":138},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":80},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":57},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":41},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":28}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":525},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":409},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":376},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":361},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":325}]}}